  "dom_id": "step-q159760",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation.  Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$",
  "worked_solution": "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{1}{4}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
